<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Achilles</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Achilles is the greatest Greek warrior of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a> in Greek legend and the central figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>. The son of the hero Peleus and the sea-nymph Thetis, he killed the Trojan prince Hector and was himself killed by an arrow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He was worshipped as a hero, notably around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> at Olbia and on the island of Leuke, and the Molossian kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> claimed descent from his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoptolemus">Neoptolemus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/achilles.jpg" alt="Achilles" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Achilles_fighting_against_Memnon_Leiden_Rijksmuseum_voor_Oudheden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Beyond the Olympians, the Greek world was full of divine powers: nymphs of springs and trees, river gods, the Fates, the Furies, and heroes, mortals of the distant past such as Heracles, Theseus and <b>Achilles</b>, who were worshipped at their tombs and could help or harm the living.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Wall painting survives in the tombs: the scenes of feasting and sport at Tarquinia, and the François Tomb at Vulci, of the 4th century BCE, which sets <b>Achilles</b>’ sacrifice of Trojan prisoners beside local heroes, among them Macstrna freeing Caelius Vibenna.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The poem opens with an invocation to the Muse, and its first word, mēnin, “wrath”, names its theme, the wrath of <b>Achilles</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The Molossian kings claimed descent from Neoptolemus, son of <b>Achilles</b>, and were called Aeacids.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-epirus">Kingdom of Epirus</a></li><li><blockquote>A self-governing polis, it worshipped Apollo Delphinios and <b>Achilles</b>, and issued early cast bronze money shaped like dolphins.</blockquote><a href="/state/olbia">Olbia</a></li><li><blockquote>Pharsalus stood on a hill above the plain of the Enipeus in southern Thessaly, in the region Greek myth linked to <b>Achilles's</b> Phthia.</blockquote><a href="/state/pharsalus">Pharsalus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.96,26.24" aria-label="Troy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5196 734 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Achilles"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5646 1004h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5646 1004h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.96,26.24">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
